<title>USA: U.S. July personal income rose 0.1 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. July personal income rose 0.1 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1996-08-30</dateline>
<text>
<p>The U.S. Commerce Department personal income and spending estimates, in seasonally adjusted annual rates.</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:   July  June-Rev from  May-Rev from   Apr</p>
<p> Income (Current Dlrs)  0.1    0.9    0.9    0.5    0.5    0.5</p>
<p>Disposable Income:</p>
<p> -- Current Dlrs	  0.1    0.8    0.9    1.4    1.4   -0.5</p>
<p> -- Chain '92 Dlrs     -0.1    0.8    0.8    1.3    1.2   -0.8</p>
<p>Consumption:</p>
<p> -- Current Dlrs	  0.2   -0.4   -0.2    0.8    0.8    0.5</p>
<p> -- Chain '92 Dlrs     Unch   -0.4   -0.2    0.6    0.6    0.2</p>
<p>Billions of Dlrs:	July  Jun-Rev from  May-Rev from  April</p>
<p> Income		   6,467  6,460  6,461  6,405  6,402  6,371</p>
<p> Disposable Income    5,607  5,601  5,601  5,556  5,553  5,478</p>
<p> Personal Outlays     5,312  5,302  5,313  5,320  5,321  5,280</p>
<p> Pers.Consumption     5,149  5,140  5,152  5,159  5,160  5,121</p>
<p> -Durable Goods	   626    634    643    648    650    632</p>
<p> -Non-Durable Goods   1,549  1,544  1,552  1,549  1,551  1,545</p>
<p> -Services		2,974  2,962  2,957  2,962  2,959  2,944</p>
<p> Personal Saving	  295    299    288    235    233    197</p>
<p> Pct Savings Rate	 5.3    5.3    5.1    4.2    4.2    3.6</p>
<p>Billions of Dlrs:	July  Jun-Rev from  May-Rev from  April</p>
<p> Wages and Salaries   3,638  3,643  3,644  3,597  3,596  3,579</p>
<p> -Manufacturing	   678    676    675    672    672    668</p>
<p> -Services		1,260  1,263  1,263  1,239  1,239  1,233</p>
<p> -Government		642    640    641    639    639    637</p>
<p> Proprietors' Income    523    520    520    517    516    511</p>
<p> -Farm			 50     49     48     47     46     43</p>
<p> -Non-Farm		  473    471    472    471    471    468</p>
<p>Wall Street economists expected July personal income to fall by 0.1 percent and spending to rise 0.2 percent.</p>
<p>The department said private wages and salaries slowed dramatically in July because a decline in average weekly hours and average hourly earnings more than offset the effect of an increase in employment.</p>
<p>The 5.3 percent savings rates in June and July were the highest monthly rates since 5.5 percent in October 1995, the department said.</p>
